**Description**
`UdpSocket::recv` silently disregards any errors surfaced by the (Linux) kernel in `SO_ERROR`. This can cause sockets to hang instead of returning an appropriate error. For example, when calling `recv` after sending to a closed port.

[Rust Playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=675bb45ca4a03153783f3067798d7563)
```rust
use std::{io, net::SocketAddr};
use tokio::net::UdpSocket;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> io::Result<()> {
    // Bind a server socket then immediately drop it to get a port we know is closed
    let server = UdpSocket::bind("127.0.0.1:0").await?;
    let target: SocketAddr = server.local_addr()?;
    drop(server);

    let sock = UdpSocket::bind("127.0.0.1:0").await?;
    sock.connect(target).await?;
    sock.send(b"ping").await?;

    let mut buf = [0; 8];
    let result = sock.recv(&mut buf).await;  // hangs infinitely
    println!("{:?}", result);

    Ok(())
}
```

The standard library `UdpSocket::recv` implementation works correctly because it uses the Linux [`recv(2)`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/recv.2.html) syscall which will return any error that occurred.

[Rust Playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=60b77c15c8dac9a2c70194c79529be97)
```rust
use std::{io, net::{SocketAddr, UdpSocket}};

fn main() -> io::Result<()> {
    // Bind a server socket then immediately drop it to get a port we know is closed
    let server = UdpSocket::bind("127.0.0.1:0")?;
    let target: SocketAddr = server.local_addr()?;
    drop(server);

    let sock = UdpSocket::bind("127.0.0.1:0")?;
    sock.connect(target)?;
    sock.send(b"ping")?;

    let mut buf = [0; 8];
    let err = sock.recv(&mut buf).unwrap_err();

    assert_eq!(err.kind(), io::ErrorKind::ConnectionRefused);

    Ok(())
}
```

Calling `read` on a Tokio `TcpStream` also works as expected and will return any errors pending in `SO_ERROR`.
